<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heraldry</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as armory</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heraldry is the discipline of the design, display, study and transmission of armorial bearings: a coat of arms on a shield, with helmet, crest and such devices as supporters, badges and mottoes, formally described in a blazon. Devices marking people and groups go back to antiquity, but regular hereditary designs developed only in the High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>; seals dating from between 1135 and 1155 show heraldic devices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 1484 England's royal heralds were incorporated into the College of Arms. Heraldry outlived the abandonment of armour in the seventeenth century and is still used by people, towns and organisations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heraldry.jpg" alt="Heraldry" width="330" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hyghalmen_Roll_Late_1400s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Symbols to represent individuals and groups are thousands of years old. Egyptian kings' names appear on emblems called serekhs, topped with the falcon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, and the Book of Numbers refers to the standards of the children of Israel. Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writers describe the shields of heroes, and the first record of a shield blazon is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>' tragedy Seven Against Thebes. The regular, hereditary designs that distinguish heraldry did not develop until the High Middle Ages.</p><p>The modern heraldic language cannot be traced to one person, time or place. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry</a>, probably commissioned about 1077, shows shields that are plain or decorated with dragons or crosses, yet no individual is shown twice with the same arms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A heraldic achievement consists of a shield of arms together with accompanying elements such as a helmet and crest, supporters, badges, banners and a motto. The shield is the one indispensable element, and strictly the term coat of arms refers to the shield alone. The crest grew out of the three-dimensional figure placed on a mounted knight's helm. A motto is a phrase describing the motivation of the bearer and is generally changed at will, except in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, where it is granted as part of the blazon.</p><p>Arms are described in a formal language called blazon, with its own vocabulary and syntax, from the Middle English blasoun, from French blason, shield. The spread of arms produced the herald, originally a messenger employed by noblemen, who learned the rank, pedigree and devices of knights and lords. As early as the late thirteenth century heralds in the service of monarchs were given the title King of Heralds, later King of Arms. By the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the tradition had diverged into German-Nordic, Gallo-British, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Eastern styles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Although heraldry arose from military need, it flourished in the tournament, which led to refinements such as elaborate tournament helms. Prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a>, cities and towns took or were granted arms with only nominal military links, and devices appeared in religious and funerary art, stonework, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stained-glass">stained glass</a> and embroidery.</p><p>Other cultures used comparable emblems, among them the Japanese mon, the tamgas of Eurasian nomads and the tughra, the monogram of each Ottoman sultan. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, the English King of Arms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s Lord Lyon King of Arms and the Chief Herald of Ireland continue to grant arms, and heraldic authorities also exist in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heraldry” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
